---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import HelpCTA from '../components/HelpCTA.astro';
import Src from '../components/Src.astro';
const title = 'Apostille Atlas';
const description = 'State-by-state apostille fees, offices, and mail versus in-person rules, copied from official secretary of state pages, plus apostille versus authentication and online notarization.';
const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebSite',
  name: 'Apostille Atlas',
  url: 'https://apostillesteps.com/',
  description,
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Atlas Guides' }
};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} schema={schema} updated="2026-11-23">
  <h1>Apostille Atlas</h1>
  <p class="lede">A state-by-state guide to U.S. apostilles and authentication certificates: the official fee, the issuing office, mail versus in-person filing, and whatever processing time that office actually publishes.</p>
  <p>An apostille is a certificate that authenticates the signature on a public document so the document can be used in another country that belongs to the 1961 Hague Apostille Convention. The Hague Conference describes the Convention as replacing the longer legalization chain with a single apostille issued by a competent authority where the document originates.<Src href="https://www.hcch.net/en/instruments/conventions/specialised-sections/apostille" /></p>
  <div class="note">We copy fees and rules from official government pages and put a source link on every claim. Offices change prices and counter hours. This is general information, not legal advice. We don't file documents ourselves, but if you'd rather hand the job off, our request form passes it to an independent apostille service.</div>
  <HelpCTA />

  <h2>State guides</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/new-york-apostille/">New York</a></h3>
      <p>$10 per document at the Department of State. Same-day walk-in service, limited to 10 documents, at five customer-service offices. No published mail turnaround.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/california-apostille/">California</a></h3>
      <p>$20 per apostille by mail; in person add $6 special handling per public official’s signature. Sacramento was processing mail received November 12, 2026, as of November 20, 2026.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/texas-apostille/">Texas</a></h3>
      <p>$15 per universal apostille ($10, capped at $100, for an adoption). Same-day in-person service for up to 10 documents; mailed requests can take up to two weeks from receipt.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/florida-apostille/">Florida</a></h3>
      <p>$10 per document, or $20 when a clerk-of-court certification needs a certificate of incumbency. Walk-in service in Tallahassee; as of November 20, 2026, apostille requests received November 16, 2026 were being processed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/massachusetts-apostille/">Massachusetts</a></h3>
      <p>$6 per document. Up to three documents at the Boston counter; mailed requests are processed within two to three weeks. Regional offices in Springfield and Fall River.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/new-jersey-apostille/">New Jersey</a></h3>
      <p>$25 standard or $40 expedited per document, ordered and prepaid online first. Standard mail service takes 12 to 20 business days; walk-in expedited service in Trenton.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/virginia-apostille/">Virginia</a></h3>
      <p>$10 per document, $5 for extra same-signer documents. Documents must be dated within 12 months. Richmond is appointment-only, and eNotary documents can’t be authenticated.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/north-carolina-apostille/">North Carolina</a></h3>
      <p>$10 per document. Walk-in service in Raleigh with no appointment; mailed requests generally take five business days longer. Foreign-language documents need a wet-signed English translation.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>How the certificate works</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/apostille-vs-authentication/">Apostille versus authentication</a></h3>
      <p>Hague Convention countries generally use an apostille. Non-Hague countries often need a different certificate, and sometimes a further step at the U.S. Department of State. States do not all issue the same kind of paper.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/online-notarization-for-apostille/">Online notarization (RON)</a></h3>
      <p>A remote online notary can notarize a document, but the apostille still comes from the state competent authority. Texas publishes a specific RON checklist; some countries still refuse online notarizations.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

